  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1: Feasibility and Design Coordination (Weeks 4-12)",
      "text": "This initial phase involves figuring out if an attached ADU is even possible on your property and then getting the design just right. Hometelligent Inc. can help you assess site constraints, local zoning, and existing utility capacity. You'll coordinate with designers and engineers to create site plans, floor plans, elevations, and structural details. This step also includes energy documentation, which is crucial for Bay Area projects. This stage typically takes several weeks, depending on design complexity and how quickly you can make decisions."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2,
      "name": "Step 2: Permit Application and Approval (Months 2-6+)",
      "text": "Once your designs are complete, it's time to submit them for planning, building, and trade permits. This is often the longest and most variable part of the timeline. The local jurisdiction reviews your plans for compliance with all codes and regulations. If your project needs a variance or special-use approval, this phase can take even longer, as hearings might only be scheduled periodically. Hometelligent coordinates this complex process, but plan-review workloads can influence total time."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3,
      "name": "Step 3: Site Preparation and Foundation (Weeks 3-6)",
      "text": "With permits in hand, construction can finally begin! This involves preparing your site, which might include demolition, excavation, and then laying the foundation for your attached ADU. Attention to detail here is critical, especially where the new foundation connects to your existing home. Structural calculations and proper interfaces are key for moisture control and stability. Weather can also play a role in this phase, potentially causing minor delays."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4,
      "name": "Step 4: Framing and Rough-Ins (Weeks 4-8)",
      "text": "This is where your ADU starts to take shape. The framing goes up, defining the rooms, and then the rough plumbing, electrical, and HVAC systems are installed. Inspections will be required at various points during this phase to ensure everything meets code before walls are closed up. Coordinating these trades efficiently is vital to keeping the project moving."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5,
      "name": "Step 5: Finishes and Interior Work (Months 2-4)",
      "text": "Once the rough-ins are approved, it's time for the interior to come alive. This includes insulation, drywall, flooring, cabinetry, lighting, plumbing fixtures, and all the final touches that make it a livable space. Integration with the existing home's aesthetic, if desired, is also managed here. Quality control is paramount in this phase, as these are the elements you'll see and use every day."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 6,
      "name": "Step 6: Final Inspections and Occupancy (Weeks 2-4)",
      "text": "The last step involves final inspections by the city or county to ensure everything is built to code. Once approved, you'll receive your certificate of occupancy, meaning your attached ADU is officially ready for use! This final approval signifies the project's completion. Hometelligent manages all final walkthroughs and necessary documentation. Ready to get started on your attached ADU? Connect with Hometelligent Inc. to discuss your project timeline and details."
    }
  ],
